 `after_llm_calls` otherwise). All four are `null` on non-timeout errors.
 
+### Unresponsive or overloaded provider
+
+A child that waits on a provider which never answers is not frozen: its model wait keeps the activity clock moving, so neither the heartbeat nor `child_timeout_seconds` ends it. The per-call limits end it instead: a stream with no chunk for the stale timeout is reconnected until `HERMES_STREAM_STALE_GIVEUP` (5) consecutive stale streams open the breaker, and an overloaded provider (HTTP 529, or a 429 `engine_overloaded`) goes through the `agent.auto_recovery_cycles` wait ladder. A provider that queues the request and keeps the stream open with keepalive comments can hold a child that way for 15 minutes or more. Two optional keys give background children their own, shorter patience without changing the parent's:
+
+```yaml
+delegation:
+  stream_stale_giveup: 1      # default: null = inherit HERMES_STREAM_STALE_GIVEUP (5)
+  auto_recovery_cycles: 0     # default: null = inherit agent.auto_recovery_cycles (5)
+```
+
+With these values a child fails after one stale window (`providers.<id>.stale_timeout_seconds`, or `providers.<id>.models.<model>.stale_timeout_seconds`, sets its length) or after its ordinary API retries on an overload, and the parent gets the provider's error.
+
 ## Failure Visibility
 
 A subagent that fails — non-retryable provider error (404/400), timeout, crash, or no usable output — is never silent:
